{"title":"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Complementary_Counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Complementary Counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Direct_and_Complementary_Counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Direct and Complementary Counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Complementary_Counting_and_PIE)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Complementary Counting and PIE)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Direct_Counting_with_Subtraction)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Direct Counting with Subtraction)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_mop_2024\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by mop 2024</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Just_2_min!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Just 2 min!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(1-16)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(1-16)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Kayla rolls four fair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-sided dice. What is the probability that at least one of the numbers Kayla rolls is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and at least two of the numbers she rolls are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/2/06264b15903e910bfbd6becee8fb05b0ff58c8fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\qquad  \\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{19}{27} \\qquad  \\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{59}{81} \\qquad  \\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{61}{81} \\qquad  \\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{7}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"398\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Complementary_Counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Complementary_Counting)\">Solution 1 (Complementary Counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Complementary Counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will subtract from one the probability that the first condition is violated and the probability that <i>only</i> the second condition is violated, being careful not to double-count the probability that both conditions are violated.\n</p><p>For the first condition to be violated, all four dice must read <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or less, which happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/1/071c42b7fcfc0996ae7f6713bbafca52f2a50b62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left( \\frac23 \\right)^4 = \\frac{16}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"95\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>For the first condition to be met but the second condition to be violated, at least one of the dice must read greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, but less than two of the dice can read greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, one of the four die must read <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, while the remaining three dice must read <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or less, which happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/3/1d3af3d9492cdcbf524e72fa0bad21005d4cdb8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${4 \\choose 1} \\left(\\frac13\\right) \\left(\\frac13\\right)^3 = 4 \\cdot \\frac13 \\cdot \\frac{1}{27} = \\frac{4}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"272\" height=\"55\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, the overall probability of meeting both conditions is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/d/4ada65bbede491ccaedcc167f5b62ceea7eca320.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\frac{16}{81} - \\frac{4}{81} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{61}{81}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"192\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Direct_and_Complementary_Counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Direct_and_Complementary_Counting)\">Solution 2 (Direct and Complementary Counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Direct and Complementary Counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dice that have values of more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The probability of getting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/0/1b0f93cb6eea4681500b9effa940925c5758fa71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^4 = \\frac{16}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"91\" height=\"46\" />, the probability of getting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/c/e6c289cb4e60ed7b9d4feacd089caa8ee6219392.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{32}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"159\" height=\"48\" />, and the probability of getting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or greater is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae555a1b224b19a5c4f27f8eb02fa0ff2ef92d9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\frac{16}{81} - \\frac{32}{81} = \\frac{11}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"146\" height=\"38\" />. \n</p><p>It is obvious that the probability of getting at least two numbers greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> if we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> numbers greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Let us calculate the probability of getting at least two numbers greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> if one die is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> by using complementary counting. We already have one die that is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the probability that a die that is less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Thus, our probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c36d87293effc3a6d109b7a76119996a331c260.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\frac{7}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"117\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, our total probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e3326886af97f1f3b2046b253869c7cb0f5cabfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{11}{27} + \\frac{7}{8}\\cdot\\frac{32}{81} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{61}{81}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"187\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy100\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Complementary_Counting_and_PIE.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Complementary_Counting_and_PIE)\">Solution 3 (Complementary Counting and PIE)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Complementary Counting and PIE)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We use Solution 1's idea of complementary counting. However, we will find the probability that the first condition is violated and the probability that the second condition is violated, then subtract the overlap (first and second condition both violated). We can do this because of PIE.\n</p><p>As in Solution 1, the probability that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee49c7f7cb95d688127aab5b3c04b5917472620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{first condition}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"119\" height=\"13\" /> is violated is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/9/2699ab2b1c76388d0071588f788fd9359934884e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^4 = \\frac{16}{81}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"100\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>The probability that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/5/5c50f88d2ac3336d92f78df75c90e077a0401993.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{second condition}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"141\" height=\"13\" /> is violated (regardless of the first condition) can be broken into two cases.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/5/555cbbe6666e1632944439bb81bcfdb74de9f77f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Case 1: exactly one number rolled is greater than 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"435\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Then, there are four choices for which die is the one greater than 2, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance for whether it reads 3, 4, 5, or 6, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/3/d43f6d510fbf809c6f1d815359163467d52c0d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"91\" height=\"46\" /> for the other three die that must read 1 or 2. The probability is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/e/afe334ba3181bc1466ac07e57377b48d5f1f1b7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right) = \\frac{8}{81}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"156\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/7/1179f29bc2028218032b58e2925f42cc11830dae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Case 2: exactly zero numbers rolled are greater than 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"461\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Then, all four dice are either 1 or 2, so the probability of this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/2141136904171237b9d815a2ecc3a2fa9f887bdf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{81}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"100\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>Now, we look at the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/a/07a9a01f4eb840beb3043ea4f182e2357a81a029.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{overlap}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />. In other words, we want to find the probability none of the rolls are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and less than two of the rolls are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We split into the same two cases again.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/a/7da3f12c73c174ba4d05ab1c246fd05a61d565b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Case 1: exactly one number rolled is greater than 2 (and none of the rolls are greater than 4)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"787\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>There are still 4 choices for this die, and it must be 3 or 4, with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. The other must read 1 or 2, with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/0/a30882cd6cb185f9e336bdae60dd7aac08f48e49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{27},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"38\" /> so this case yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/8/3a84eff57c7fc5f45be3f116cf856069a9ae82d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right) = \\frac{4}{81}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"156\" height=\"45\" /> \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/6/076c235a9c4524dca009ca10709b316ca67dab77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Case 2: exactly zero numbers rolled are greater than 2 (and none of the rolls are greater than 4)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"814\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The first statement implies the second statement, so this case yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/5/5d566f72db7c1e787d2817d1023c750de6593928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> as from our previous Case 2.\n</p><p>Now we've got everything! Our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/8/2889fa8407152f4ba0fc8ffde20522ea43120075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - (\\frac{16}{81} + (\\frac{8}{81} + \\frac{1}{81}) - (\\frac{4}{81} + \\frac{1}{81})) = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{61}{81}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"373\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~sirswagger21\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Direct_Counting_with_Subtraction.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Direct_Counting_with_Subtraction)\">Solution 4 (Direct Counting with Subtraction)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Direct Counting with Subtraction)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let X be the number of rolls greater than 4 (i.e. 5 or 6), and Y be the number of rolls greater than 2 (i.e. 3, 4, 5, or 6). We want to compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/c/b4c2352829a13a25d984947ede1e459f6c3694e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt;= 1 and Y &gt;= 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"187\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>We first compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c033d6460a9d33b9ef55607c2d261fea3369de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt;= 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />. The complement is that no roll is greater than 4, so every roll is in {1,2,3,4}. The probability of this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/1/7211f4224e39a4df09aa1cd6dab22e194e4e4aba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4/6)^4 = 16/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"120\" height=\"21\" />. Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/e/2ce871e5f306eb580328e5cfb8425d4d6fa8add1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt;= 1) = 1 - 16/81 = 65/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"265\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>This already guarantees Y &gt;= 1, but we still need to enforce Y &gt;= 2. Therefore, we subtract the outcomes where X &gt;= 1 but Y = 1.\n</p><p>The only way for X &gt;= 1 and Y = 1 to occur is if exactly one die is greater than 2, and that die is also greater than 4. In other words, one die is 5 or 6, and the other three dice are 1 or 2.\n</p><p>There are 4 choices for which die is &gt;=5, and that die has probability 2/6 = 1/3. The other three dice must be in {1,2}, each with probability 1/3. Therefore, the probability of this “bad” case is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/e/7eeb3e120f2ca4a98337108e154af763cac82207.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 * (1/3) * (1/3)^3 = 4/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"196\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, subtracting gives\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/2/0627a750640a3e186984c85703d74732c8c3ff86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt;= 1 and Y &gt;= 2) = 65/81 - 4/81 = 61/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"385\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/1/51111e78c0387b05f9cf3f40f5bf3110ea3a57bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ 61/81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"94\" height=\"32\" />.\n~FastOne\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_mop_2024\">Video Solution by mop 2024</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by mop 2024\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ezGvZgBLe8k&amp;t=171s\">https://youtu.be/ezGvZgBLe8k&amp;t=171s</a>\n</p><p>~r00tsOfUnity\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Just_2_min.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Just_2_min!)\">Video Solution (Just 2 min!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Just 2 min!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m-rF2xwDLGM\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m-rF2xwDLGM</a>\n</p><p>-<i> ~Education, the Study of Everything </i>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.281-16.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(1-16)\">Video Solution(1-16)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(1-16)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/SCwQ9jUfr0g\">https://youtu.be/SCwQ9jUfr0g</a>\n</p><p>~~Hayabusa1\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B\">2022 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_1\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_2\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw